    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d625">

                <pb facs="0667" n="617" sameAs="frus:frus1894/pg617"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Muruaga to Mr. Gresham.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of Spain</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1894-03-25"><hi rendition="#italic">March 25,
                        1894</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: A cablegram from the
                    governor general of Cuba informs me of the fact that a mob composed of several
                    hundred roughs, armed with clubs and revolvers, attacked lately the Spanish
                    cigar-makers of the tobacco manufactory “Rosa Española” in Key West, obliging
                    them under fear of personal violence to stop work. The interference of the
                    police could barely avoid bloodshed.</p>
                <p>It is intimated that should work be resumed to-morrow the same lawbreakers
                    threaten wholesale murder.</p>
                <p>Would it be too much asking of your kindness to have matter recommended at once
                    to the governor of the State of Florida, on whom devolves the authority to
                    guarantee protection and security to foreign residents?</p>
                <p>Very sincerely, yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">E. de Muruaga.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
